Canonical Retained-Document Streaming — S7B
===============================================================================

Implementation: B1-B6, rebased onto the S7A base recorded below.
Historical growth measurements: f33df6d8fdebbe670f2f05067ceb54891f835b26.
B6 closeout: API and handoff implemented; exact-head qualification lives on PR #826.
A completed checkpoint document does not certify pending CI or review.
PR: https://github.com/mech-lang/mech/pull/826 (draft).
Base: S7A #825, f6fa1075d0ed75d15413d2f31813486c70206fa7.
Branch: codex/syntax-s7b-streaming -> codex/syntax-s7-document-root.

1. Scope and authority
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DocumentStream retains a canonical document while valid UTF-8 text arrives.
The document grammar, recursive rule continuations, recognition, recovery, typed
syntax, and semantic consumer boundary have one implementation. Final one-shot
parsing drives those same owners with final input. Frozen grammar witnesses and
structural hashes provide an independent reference beyond differential tests.

The grammar inventory remains 520/539. This work adds no productions, alternate
AST, compatibility parser, semantic capability, FSM scheduling, or production
caller switch. S8 performs production integration and removal of retiring paths.
S2/S3 remain frozen. Shared canonical execution changes belong in this S7B diff.

Production parsing uses safe, deterministic core/alloc machinery. Transports,
UTF-8 byte buffering, I/O, threads, clocks, and async scheduling remain external.
Retained-document streaming keeps accepted source and syntax inspectable; its
storage grows with the document. Callers control retention of historical views.

2. Public lifecycle and scheduling
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DocumentStream::new(document, config) starts empty. with_limits additionally
sets StreamLimits { max_source_bytes, max_parser_work }. ParseConfig retains the
canonical fuel, event, diagnostic, recovery, and nesting limits.

Open -> Finishing -> Finished
Open/Finishing -> Limited
Open/Finishing -> Cancelled

append(text, allowance) accepts an entire valid UTF-8 string or returns an error.
SourceLimit rejects oversized input before append allocation; accepted source,
revision, interpretation, and work remain unchanged. Empty append is a no-op,
even when accepted input still needs processing. Closed streams reject appends.

advance(allowance) processes accepted input without declaring final EOF.
StreamProgress::NeedInput means the retained owner needs additional source.
NeedsProcessing means the owner can continue when given another allowance.
Neither outcome means syntax failure. Limited reports hard resource exhaustion.

finish(allowance) declares final EOF once and changes state to Finishing. Call
advance while progress is NeedsProcessing; Finished identifies the final result.
Repeated finish on Finished preserves identity and performs no repeated work.
An established syntax error can recover before final EOF. An unfinished string,
operator prefix, grapheme, alternative, or physical closer stays suspended until
its decision is established or finish supplies final EOF.

cancel stops live processing and retains every accepted byte. It does not supply
final EOF or allow materialize. Cancellation is terminal for edits and appends.

The allowance measures canonical continuation/scanner/materialization transitions,
not CPU instructions or a wall-clock deadline. A Unicode transition supplies at
most one forward and one preceding scalar to the pinned segmentation cursor;
extra backward context has separately charged transitions. Allocation, retained
source lookup, journal path copying, and discarded volume have additional work
counters. append must also copy/index the incoming chunk before processing it.

max_parser_work caps cumulative live plus preview continuation transitions.
It does not cap StreamWork::total(), explicit export, or source insertion work.
Neither appends, edits, speculative rewinds, nor invalidated previews replenish
that session budget. Final limited-envelope cleanup is explicit export work;
canonical fuel/event/recovery/diagnostic constraints still apply to its output.

3. Publication, diagnostics, and identity
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every StreamUpdate contains progress, accepted_bytes, syntax and diagnostic
StreamChange suffix deltas, a shared StreamView, and cumulative StreamWork.
For each delta, replace [from, old_len) with [from, new_len) from the new journal.
The baseline is the immediately preceding returned update, including every
NeedsProcessing result. Apply both deltas before calling advance again; use the
same loop for append and finish. Dropping intermediate updates is not coalescing.
A consumer that skips updates must explicitly resynchronize from the full view.
The executable DocumentStream example shows the consume-before-advance loop.
A speculative rewind can replace an earlier suffix. No update enumerates the
unchanged prefix or builds the complete node/token/restart indexes.

StreamView exposes source, parsed_through, pending_source, event_count/event,
events/events_from, diagnostic_count/diagnostic, and completed_node(event_index).
The root role is Document. Incomplete parents remain open on the canonical event
tape; completed_node returns existing SyntaxNode values for materialized children.
Bounded node lookup supports existing typed access without full-tree export.
completed_node_with_work also returns actual journal lookup steps, allowing a
caller to account for requested access without mutating live parser counters.
A complete view traversal or node.text() is caller-requested work, proportional
to the data requested, and is outside automatic ingestion accounting.

Historical views preserve their source, journals, and completed node Arcs.
Diagnostics bind absolute anchors to the view's revision when read. Diagnostic
suffixes may change when alternatives resolve or final EOF establishes recovery.
Completed children retained by the selected parse keep their node identities
through subsequent appends and final materialization. Rewound candidates do not
promise identity continuity. Node identity is scoped to its document session.

StreamIdentity includes document, revision, interpretation generation, state,
and StreamInterpretation (Live, FinitePreview, Finalized, Limited, Cancelled).
Source revision alone is insufficient for caching syntax. Finalization and
limited-envelope export can change interpretation without changing source bytes.
Limited materialization publishes the finalized journal baseline under a new
identity so subsequent edit deltas refer to that publication. materialize returns
no delta: after Limited export, explicitly replace the consumer baseline from
stream.view() before applying further updates. Unchanged ordinary view reads do
not advance the baseline. Retain diagnostic anchors relative to the current view
revision and bind them on read, rather than rewriting all settled diagnostics.

4. Explicit previews, exports, and session transitions
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

preview() performs explicit finite-prefix canonical parsing and returns a
ProvisionalSnapshot. It shares the remaining session continuation budget, reports
its separate preview_work and preview_parser_work, and caches unchanged requests.
It never changes live alternatives, events, grammar fuel, or recovery decisions.
Preview node issuance shares the document ID sequence, preventing preview/live
ID aliasing. FinitePreview always remains provisional, even if strictly clean.
Under exhausted limits it contains the canonical limited envelope; it does not
promise the unlimited one-shot result. Repeated invalidated previews may require
full prefix parsing and are excluded from ordinary-publication growth claims.

materialize() is available only after Finished or Limited. It explicitly exports
SyntaxSnapshot, including the complete root, diagnostics, node/token indexes,
and restart metadata. Repeated calls share the cached Arc. Limited export keeps
the accepted suffix lossless and charges cleanup separately. Exported source and
public views do not carry the private parser lookup counter; external reads cannot
mutate the live accounting. A limited or recovered snapshot is not execution-ready.

apply_edits(edits, allowance) validates ordered, nonoverlapping UTF-8 ranges and
checks resulting source capacity before constructing storage or indexing inserted
text. It discards suspended owners/caches and restarts canonical parsing on the
edited source. Historical views remain valid; old identities are not reconciled
into this new interpretation. The returned deltas replace the old journals.
full_document_restarts and edit_restart_work charge this transition. Arbitrary
edits may rebuild source storage and line metadata; no sublinear edit claim applies.
Adjacent edits at one old-source boundary contribute to the complete projected
rescan window, including CR/LF text split across separate insertions. Rebuilding
that window advances one retained piece cursor rather than restarting a piece
search for each byte, and the conservative edit charge includes edit multiplicity.

into_session() transfers a finalized or limited materialized snapshot, identity
issuance, interpretation generation, and configuration to DocumentSession and
returns accumulated work. The generation includes any limited-export invalidation.
If a caller retains the materialized Arc, the required snapshot clone charges
node/token indexes, restart and diagnostic records, nested vectors, and owned
UTF-8 payload bytes. Otherwise ownership transfers without that clone.

DocumentSession::into_stream() preserves source/configuration/ID issuance and
establishes a new retained parse. It reports existing source storage immediately
and one full_document_restart. It advances the retained interpretation generation
before exposing the new stream, even if the session received no edits. Document
and source revision remain unchanged; a new interpretation cannot reuse cached
node associations from a previous stream. Call advance to process that accepted source, or
append additional text. This explicit transition does not reconcile old session
nodes into the new stream. Finite DocumentSession edit/reconciliation semantics
remain available to consumers that continue to own a finite document.

5. Retained storage and canonical owners
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TextSnapshot pieces and line starts use balanced persistent sequences. Append
copies only the new text, scans its line endings, and path-copies storage nodes.
CR/LF split repair replaces a shared line-start path without changing old views.
Byte/chunk lookup visits a logarithmic path; sequential exports traverse retained
storage in linear time. LineIndex::line_starts explicitly exports a Vec.

ParserState owns journals, open markers, rules, cursor bounds, resources, and
materialization state independently of input borrows. Resume extends only bounds
marked as an input frontier; fixed embedded ranges remain fixed. Generated
canonical document phases and all 80 recursive Phase 2I rule entries retain
suspension, selected alternatives, delimiters, and rule-owned recovery.
Executable fences feed the child owner only the safe prefix until their physical
closer or final EOF seals it. No newline is treated as a universal commit point.

Base and delimiter traversal retain Unicode cursor state between graphemes.
Literal comparison rejects nonmatching non-ASCII prefixes without repeatedly
walking regional-indicator context; ASCII probes preserve grapheme-frontier waits.
The pinned Unicode implementation remains the segmentation authority.

Persistent event/diagnostic journals share prefixes and count changed path copies.
Completed-node materialization is scheduled under the same continuation allowance;
token bytes are hashed incrementally. GreenChildren preserves the canonical logical
child order and original structural hash formula while sharing growing children.
There are no synthetic grouping nodes or replacement certification hashes.

6. Work accounting and supported performance claims
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

StreamWork reports source copying/indexing/storage allocations and actual parser
source-tree lookup visits; continuation transitions/resumes; journal mutations,
allocations and discards; materialized nodes, hashed token bytes, child storage and
reuse; publications; preview/export work; and explicit edit restarts.
Checkpoint rewinds, rewound bytes and cached replay bytes are reported separately.

StreamWork::total() sums charged categories rather than summing every descriptive
counter twice. preview_parser_work is already included in preview_work. Export
accounting is a conservative record/payload-volume charge, not a cycle count.
Allocator headers, caller-owned input creation and explicit public traversal are
outside the owned-body byte fields. Peak source pieces/bytes, events, open markers,
rule depth, document frames, and pending materialization describe components;
they are not a measurement of all resident bytes or every recursive frame.

The growth gate uses fixed small UTF-8 chunks at N=64,128,256,512, verifies complete
source and canonical final tree/diagnostics, checks resource caps did not truncate
the workload, and includes final grammar draining. It runs ingestion alone and
with normal views, metered completed-node access, and retained historical views.
Each doubling must stay <=3x charged work. Explicit materialize/export is measured
separately. A six-byte append to increasingly large settled prefixes has its own
<=3x bound across an eightfold prefix increase.

Qualified families are strings, nested expressions, inert/executable fences,
combining clusters, regional indicators, malformed string tails, and late brace
selection. These measured families do not establish universal linear parsing for
all grammar/input combinations. Arbitrary edits, repeated finite previews, full
exports, and caller-requested full traversals have separate proportional costs.

The frozen B1 S7A baseline remains in s7b-streaming-baseline.json. It measured
partial parser/event/reconciliation accounting and four failing positive gates.
Current complete counters are not directly comparable as instruction counts.
Do not reinterpret historical B1 numbers as full end-to-end execution costs.

